154
00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:30.471
Diagnose a failure by tracing the first
violated contract, not

155
00:17:30.471 --> 00:17:35.941
by treating every symptom as a model
failure. A public

156
00:17:35.941 --> 00:17:41.412
health success establishes reachability
only. A 401 from an authenticated

157
00:17:41.412 --> 00:17:46.882
endpoint establishes rejection for that
supplied credential, not universal

158
00:17:46.882 --> 00:17:52.353
authentication coverage. A client timeout
establishes that the client deadline

159
00:17:52.353 --> 00:17:57.824
expired; it does not establish server
cancellation or absence of

160
00:17:57.824 --> 00:18:03.294
work. A stream ending with DONE and text
supports that

161
00:18:03.294 --> 00:18:08.765
observed exchange, but not every streaming
edge case. A wrong

162
00:18:08.765 --> 00:18:14.235
model revision is an identity and
readiness failure even if

163
00:18:14.235 --> 00:18:19.706
generation succeeds. An occupied port is a
lifecycle setup failure,

164
00:18:19.706 --> 00:18:25.176
while an unrelated PID on that port is an

165
00:18:25.176 --> 00:18:30.647
ownership safety failure. The qualified
lifecycle negatives include existing-file

166
00:18:30.647 --> 00:18:36.118
preservation, corrupted archive, occupied
port, and refusal to stop an

167
00:18:36.118 --> 00:18:41.588
unrelated PID. The positive sequence is
start, same-key identity, owned

168
00:18:41.588 --> 00:18:47.059
stop, restart, same-key identity, and
owned stop. Diagnose the cause,

169
00:18:47.059 --> 00:18:52.529
preserve the failed evidence, choose hold,
repair, and rerun the

170
00:18:52.529 --> 00:18:58.000
smallest affected gate.

171
00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:04.250
Checkpoint. A client deadline expires
after the server has accepted

172
00:19:04.250 --> 00:19:10.500
a request. The client then reconnects
successfully. What can you

173
00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:16.750
claim, what must remain unknown, and what
evidence should you

174
00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:23.000
collect before calling this recovery?

175
00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:42.455
You can claim that the client deadline
expired and that

176
00:20:42.455 --> 00:20:46.909
a later client request recovered, if that
later request has

177
00:20:46.909 --> 00:20:51.364
its own observed evidence. You must not
claim that the

178
00:20:51.364 --> 00:20:55.818
server cancelled the first request, that
no work occurred, or

179
00:20:55.818 --> 00:21:00.273
that the system has a general cancellation
guarantee. Collect server-owned

180
00:21:00.273 --> 00:21:04.727
cancellation telemetry before making that
claim. A common wrong answer

181
00:21:04.727 --> 00:21:09.182
calls reconnect success proof that the
timed-out work stopped. That

182
00:21:09.182 --> 00:21:13.636
confuses client observation with server
state and can make retries

183
00:21:13.636 --> 00:21:18.091
multiply work. Hold any cancellation or
retry conclusion, preserve both

184
00:21:18.091 --> 00:21:22.545
receipts, classify the timeout, and rerun
the relevant bounded recovery

185
00:21:22.545 --> 00:21:27.000
case.

186
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:33.188
Compare adapters at the application
contract boundary. The offline exercise

187
00:21:33.188 --> 00:21:39.375
